.TH CHTTPSVR_SET_ENGINE_NUM_REACTOR_THREADS 3 "2026-07-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_set_engine_num_reactor_threads \- set the OS thread count for the shared engine's reactor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_set_engine_num_reactor_threads(size_t num_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets how many OS threads the shared ccol_event_loop reactor gives to
its own polling and dispatch.  It follows the num_reactor_threads
parameter of ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs.  See cthreadcomm.h.
A value of 1 means that one thread both polls and dispatches inline.
Any larger value means one dedicated polling thread plus
(num_threads - 1) dispatch worker threads.

The reactor uses exactly 1 thread by default.  You get the default
when you never call this function, and when you call it with
num_threads == 0.  A larger num_threads starts that many OS threads
instead.  A value of 0 gives you the default of 1 again.

This function sets a value that the reactor takes at construction
time, the same way as chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs.  You can
call it only before the first chttpsvr_start call in the process.  You
can also call it again after the reactor stops completely, that is,
after chttpsvr_engine_wait returns, and before the next
chttpsvr_start.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_not_permitted when the reactor already runs.  Stop it first in
                  that case.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The default is 1, and not a CPU count that the library detects,
because 1 is faster for the common case:

  Plain HTTP with reused connections, which is the typical traffic of
  a browser or an API client: one thread gives the best throughput.
  The dispatch phase of plain HTTP has almost no CPU-bound work in
  it, because a header parse is fast.  More threads therefore only
  add the cost of a hand-off, and there is nothing to run in
  parallel.

  TLS with reused connections, which is the typical HTTPS traffic
  after you count the connection pool of a client: this is a real
  trade, and not a clean win either way.  One thread gives lower
  throughput.  But it gives a clearly better and steadier p99 latency
  than more than one dispatch thread.  Most requests on a TLS
  connection take the same cheap steady-state path as plain HTTP.
  Only the handshake of the connection pays the expensive part, and
  that cost spreads across every request that the connection then
  serves.

  TLS with no connection reuse at all, where every request pays a new
  handshake: more than one dispatch thread gives both higher
  throughput and lower p99 latency.  This is a deliberately extreme
  case, and not typical traffic.  The asymmetric-crypto cost of a TLS
  handshake is the private-key operation of the server.  That is real
  CPU-bound work, and it gains from a spread across cores when there
  is enough of it.

A larger num_threads is worth its cost in one case: a steady churn of
connections together with TLS.  In that case many separate clients
each open a connection for only one request, or a few, before it
closes.  A large part of the total traffic therefore pays the CPU
cost of a handshake, instead of spreading it out.  This is real for
some deployments.  Examples are a public API that takes many one-off
anonymous clients, a gateway for IoT devices where unreliable
networks cause frequent reconnects, and a webhook receiver that many
different external services call.  But it is the less common shape
overall.  Most HTTP client software pools and reuses connections,
exactly to avoid a repeated handshake cost.  This is true of
browsers, of most serious HTTP client libraries, and of the
chttpclient of this library.  A server behind a load balancer or a
reverse proxy often never sees raw handshake churn from the public
internet at all.  A deployment that knows that its own traffic has a
lot of churn should raise num_threads.  This function exists for that
override.
.fi
